                <pb facs="0085" n="73" sameAs="frus:frus1946v08/pg73"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851G.00/12–1946: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Saigon</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reed</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Saigon</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-12-19T17:00:00+07:00">December 19, 1946—5
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received December 20—3:06 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>490. Since visit Moffat and <hi rendition="#italic">New York Times</hi>
                    correspondents, pro-Vietnam press publishes almost daily critical articles
                    regarding US and its policy, lack of policy, vis-à-vis French-Vietnam
                    dispute—also references to third party intervention with inference that US will
                    eventually seek economic benefits from this dispute. In mentioning tenor of
                    press, long time resident of French Indochina deplores US not taking more
                    positive stand in present dispute and warns failure French-Vietnam negotiations
                    and apparent indifference US can only drive Annamites into Soviet sphere of
                    influence.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Reed</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
